"# Chapter 8 — Isolated vs shared state (a dialect-boundary chapter)

The other dialect this book was written across runs inside **Curator**, where a **cart** is a self-contained program and *modules* are bags of verbs that carts import — sometimes with their own per-cart state, sometimes shared across carts. That whole layer is a Curator/Sakura concept. **Motoi is not Curator**, and Motoi has no `import`, no `(module ...)` form, and no `:shared` scoping. Read this chapter for its real, dialect-independent lesson: *isolated state versus shared state*, which Motoi expresses cleanly with closures.

## 8.1 Two instances, each with its own state

The other dialect's point — two carts importing the same `counter` module each get their own count — is, in Motoi, just two closures made from the same maker. Each closure seals its own `n`:

```scheme
(define (make-counter)
  (let ((n 0))
    (lambda () (set! n (+ n 1)) n)))

(define counter-a (make-counter))
(define counter-b (make-counter))

(counter-a) (counter-a)   ; a advances twice
(counter-b)               ; b advances once
(list 'a-then-b)          ; (values below come from the two calls)
;; counter-a is now at 2, counter-b at 1 — separate state
```

Two counters from one maker, no shared state between them. That is *isolation*, achieved with a closure rather than a per-cart module instance. Isolation is the sane default: separate closures cannot trample each other.

## 8.2 Explicit sharing — one instance, many holders

If you *want* several parts of a program to share state — the other dialect's `:shared #t` module — you make *one* closure and hand it to everyone. They now all poke the same sealed `n`:

```scheme
(define shared-counter (make-counter))

(define (bump-from-here) (shared-counter))
(define (bump-from-there) (shared-counter))

(bump-from-here)
(bump-from-there)
(shared-counter)
;; ⇒ 3   ; all three calls advanced the SAME counter
```

One instance, three callers, shared state. Sharing is opt-in — you got it by *choosing* to pass one closure around, exactly as `:shared #t` is opt-in in the other dialect. Sharing is powerful and risky in equal measure: it lets parts cooperate, but it couples them.

## 8.3 A shared coordinator in practice

The other dialect's shared music-player — where whoever plays a song makes the others quiet down — is, in Motoi, a shared closure that keeps a subscriber list and notifies it. `make-parameter` and `for-each` are real Motoi verbs, already loaded:

```scheme
(define (make-player)
  (let ((subscribers '()) (song #f))
    (lambda (msg . args)
      (case msg
        ((subscribe) (set! subscribers (cons (car args) subscribers)) 'ok)
        ((play)      (set! song (car args))
                     (for-each (lambda (s) (s 'now-playing song)) subscribers)
                     song)
        ((stop)      (set! song #f)
                     (for-each (lambda (s) (s 'stopped)) subscribers)
                     'stopped)))))

(define player (make-player))
(define log '())
(player 'subscribe (lambda (event . rest) (set! log (cons event log))))
(player 'play \"anthem\")
(reverse log)
;; ⇒ (now-playing)
```

Everyone who holds `player` shares its subscriber list and its current song — the same cooperation the shared module provided, built from one closure passed around.

## 8.4 System resources — there is one, so hold one reference

The other dialect models single system resources (a clock, a filesystem, an audio output) as always-shared `motoi/system/*` modules. Motoi has no such module namespace; a single resource is simply a single value you create once and pass where needed — the section 8.2 pattern. There is nothing special to import.

## 8.5 The isolation guarantee — from lexical scope

The strong guarantee — *nothing cart A does to its counter affects cart B's* — is, in Motoi, the airtight guarantee of lexical scope. A closure's `n` is reachable only through that closure. No other code, however buggy, can reach in and corrupt it. This is stronger than an honor system: it is enforced by the language.

## 8.6 When to share

The rules of thumb survive the translation intact:

- **Share** (pass one instance around) when the state represents a single resource only one of should exist — an output device, a connection, a global tally.
- **Do not share** (make a fresh instance per user) when the state is logic each part uses independently — a counter, a validator, a formatter.
- **Do not share if in doubt.** Isolation is safer; you can start sharing later; you cannot stop sharing without breaking whoever came to depend on it.

## 8.7 What Cortex can record

The other dialect logs per-cart `module-loaded` events. Motoi has no such events (no modules, no carts in the language). But if your program wants to record who is using what, Cortex is real and you write the facts yourself:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'counter-holders '(here there))
(cortex/recall 'counter-holders)
```

That is the honest Motoi shape of \"who is using this\": facts your program chooses to record.

## 8.8 Try it

Build `make-counter` as in section 8.1. Make two counters and advance them different amounts — confirm they hold separate state. Then make one counter and pass it to two helpers — confirm they share it. That is isolated-versus-shared in Motoi, with no `import`, no `:shared`, and no cart layer in sight.

Chapter 9 covers hygiene — what code can and cannot see — which in Motoi is, again, plain lexical scope. Turn the page.")
